## Summary

This milestone completes the substantive customer segmentation analysis for Project 03.

It moves from the validated customer-feature foundation into feature assessment, final segment design, held-out behavioural validation, sensitivity testing and employer-facing analytical outputs.

The project uses a fixed 31 May 2011 snapshot. Segment definitions use only information available by that date, with June-November 2011 reserved for subsequent-behaviour validation.

## What this PR adds

* profiles customer-feature distributions, concentration and redundancy;
* investigates the separate reactivation characteristics of historical-only customers;
* builds an enriched customer segmentation feature layer;
* defines eight mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive customer segments;
* validates the segments against six months of held-out purchasing behaviour;
* tests sensitivity to exact duplicates, Manual financial entries and extreme customers;
* creates final segment summary and CRM-action outputs;
* creates four portfolio visualisations;
* updates the README and data-quality documentation;
* adds detailed segmentation and validation methodology.

## Final segment design

The 4,908 eligible customers are assigned to:

* High-value active
* High-value at risk
* Core repeat
* Recent low-frequency
* Cooling low-frequency
* Drifting
* High-value lapsed
* Lapsed

Behavioural high value is defined using the top fifth of 12-month observed net sales, with an empirical boundary of £1,898.51.

Historical-only customers are ranked separately using prior observed value, with a high-value boundary of £552.40.

## Held-out validation

Across the six-month validation period:

* 2,549 of 4,908 customers make at least one qualifying future purchase;
* overall future purchase rate is 51.9%;
* High-value active customers have a 91.8% future purchase rate;
* Core repeat customers have an 81.3% rate;
* Recent low-frequency customers have a 60.0% rate;
* Cooling low-frequency customers have a 49.2% rate;
* Drifting customers have a 35.1% rate;
* High-value lapsed customers show 21.4% observed reactivation versus 12.6% for other Lapsed customers.

High-value active customers represent 16.4% of the eligible population but account for 71.7% of positive held-out observed customer value.

These results are interpreted as descriptive/predictive differentiation, not causal evidence of CRM treatment effects.

## Robustness

The main segmentation is stable to the material data-treatment assumptions identified during profiling:

* removing 11,812 ambiguous exact duplicate rows changes 6 customer assignments (0.12%);
* excluding Manual monetary effects changes 4 assignments (0.08%);
* removing the highest 1% of behavioural customers by snapshot value preserves the main held-out purchase-rate ordering.

## Final QA completed

Before marking this PR ready for review:

* final visuals were manually reviewed and refined;
* public-writing and cross-document consistency QA was completed;
* the project was standardised and fully rerun under Python 3.13.14;
* dependency versions were pinned;
* the complete raw-data-to-output workflow was reproduced successfully;
* a severe SQLite reconciliation performance issue was identified and corrected without changing the analytical result;
* UTF-8, public/private and Git hygiene checks were completed;
* final reproduction instructions were added.

## Post-merge publication checks

After merge to `main`:

* verify the deployed GitHub Pages case study;
* check all Pages images and technical links;
* complete/update repository About, website and topics;
* clean up the completed feature branch;
* synchronise the final Project Sources.

## Purpose
Promote final Chapter 7 — 「法則は構造置換を越えない」 — to source-bounded dissertation prose centered on MLTR's proved transport/repair/history conditions rather than a generic statement that laws may fail under change.

## Exact theorem chain
Using MLTR snapshot `d9e23d27c385759b9e1fea93a556f30618122fe1`:
1. inherited carried partition is exact iff output, legal-action rows, and successor carried labels are constant within every carried target fiber;
2. failure has a finite within-fiber obstruction;
3. iterative source-relative refinement gives the unique coarsest exact target repair;
4. repair state/bit increase is minimal among exact descriptions retaining source provenance;
5. one route-independent inherited terminal law exists iff all root-to-terminal paths induce the same complete carried terminal map;
6. otherwise one immutable history mode per distinct carried map is necessary and sufficient.

## Anti-overclaim boundary
